  "foaf:name": "Arctic Sea Ice Management",
  "sdo:name": "Arctic Sea Ice Management",
  "sdo:description": "Real Ice is researching whether controlled ice thickening can become a safe, effective, and scalable method to help preserve Arctic sea ice. Sea ice regulates Earth’s climate by reflecting solar radiation, supports Arctic biodiversity, and sustains Indigenous communities—yet is disappearing rapidly, with ice-free summers projected as early as the 2030s. Since 2023, we have conducted small-scale Arctic field trials showing promising early results. Our current focus is rigorous research and impact assessment—combining field testing, lab analysis, climate modelling, and Indigenous knowledge—to evaluate potential benefits, risks, and feasibility. In collaboration with Indigenous organisations, academic partners, and industry, we aim to establish whether autonomous, zero-emission technology adapted from proven ice-thickening methods could be responsibly developed at scale to slow sea-ice loss and support Arctic ecosystems and communities.",
